## Optimistic Locking with JPA @Version

```java
@Entity
public class Product {
    @Id
    private Long id;
    private String name;
    private int stockQuantity;

    @Version  // JPA manages this automatically
    private Long version;  // incremented on every update
}

// Repository layer — no explicit lock needed
@Transactional
public void decrementStock(Long productId) {
    Product p = repo.findById(productId).orElseThrow();
    if (p.getStockQuantity() < 1) throw new OutOfStockException();
    p.setStockQuantity(p.getStockQuantity() - 1);
    // JPA generates: UPDATE product SET stock=?, version=6 WHERE id=1 AND version=5
    // If another tx updated it (version is now 6), this UPDATE matches 0 rows
    // → JPA throws OptimisticLockException
}
```

## Pessimistic Locking with JPA @Lock

```java
@Repository
public interface ProductRepository extends JpaRepository<Product, Long> {
    @Lock(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)  // SELECT ... FOR UPDATE
    @Query("SELECT p FROM Product p WHERE p.id = :id")
    Optional<Product> findByIdForUpdate(@Param("id") Long id);
}

@Transactional
public void processOrder(Long productId) {
    // Lock acquired here; other transactions block until this commits
    Product p = repo.findByIdForUpdate(productId).orElseThrow();
    p.setStockQuantity(p.getStockQuantity() - 1);
    // Lock released when @Transactional method ends
}
```

## Decision Guide

| Scenario | Locking Strategy |
|----------|-----------------|
| Low contention (user editing own profile) | Optimistic |
| High contention (seat reservation, inventory) | Pessimistic |
| Long read-then-write workflows | Optimistic with retry |
| Financial transactions | Pessimistic |

## Deadlock Scenario

```
Transaction A:  LOCK account 100 → waiting for account 200
Transaction B:  LOCK account 200 → waiting for account 100
→ Circular wait → deadlock!
```

## Prevention: Consistent Lock Ordering

```java
@Transactional
public void transfer(Long fromId, Long toId, BigDecimal amount) {
    // Always lock in ascending ID order — prevents circular wait
    Long firstId  = Math.min(fromId, toId);
    Long secondId = Math.max(fromId, toId);

    Account first  = repo.findByIdForUpdate(firstId).orElseThrow();
    Account second = repo.findByIdForUpdate(secondId).orElseThrow();

    // Now safe to identify which is from/to
    Account from = fromId.equals(firstId) ? first : second;
    Account to   = fromId.equals(firstId) ? second : first;

    from.debit(amount);
    to.credit(amount);
}
```

## Retry on Deadlock

```java
@Retryable(
    value = {DeadlockLoserDataAccessException.class},
    maxAttempts = 3,
    backoff = @Backoff(delay = 50, multiplier = 2)
)
@Transactional
public void processOrder(Long orderId) {
    // Business logic that might deadlock
}

@Recover
public void handleDeadlock(DeadlockLoserDataAccessException ex, Long orderId) {
    log.error("Deadlock retries exhausted for order {}", orderId, ex);
    throw new ServiceException("Unable to process order due to high contention");
}
```

## SKIP LOCKED — Queue Processing Without Deadlocks

```sql
-- Each worker picks unclaimed tasks without blocking each other
SELECT * FROM tasks
WHERE status = 'pending'
ORDER BY priority DESC, created_at
LIMIT 10
FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED;  -- skips rows locked by other workers
```

## SQL Savepoints

```sql
BEGIN;

INSERT INTO orders (customer_id, amount) VALUES (1, 500);

SAVEPOINT before_risky_step;

INSERT INTO order_items (order_id, product_id)
VALUES (1, 9999);  -- might fail if product 9999 doesn't exist

-- If above fails:
ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT before_risky_step;
-- Order insert is still intact! Only the risky part was rolled back.

INSERT INTO order_items (order_id, product_id) VALUES (1, 100);  -- safe fallback

COMMIT;  -- commits: order + safe item insert
```

## Spring Propagation Types

```java
// REQUIRED (default): joins existing or creates new
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRED)
public void placeOrder() {
    orderRepo.save(order);
    notificationService.send(order);  // runs in SAME transaction
}

// REQUIRES_NEW: always new independent transaction
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
public void logAudit(String event) {
    // This commits independently
    // Even if outer tx rolls back, audit log is saved
    auditRepo.save(new AuditEntry(event));
}

// NESTED: uses savepoint — rollback only affects inner
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NESTED)
public void sendEmailNotification(Order order) {
    // If email fails and we rollback here, order is unaffected
    emailService.send(order.getCustomerEmail(), order);
}
```

## Propagation Quick Reference

| Propagation | Outer Exists | Outer Absent |
|-------------|-------------|-------------|
| REQUIRED | Join outer | Create new |
| REQUIRES_NEW | Suspend outer, create new | Create new |
| NESTED | Savepoint within outer | Create new |
| NOT_SUPPORTED | Suspend outer, run non-tx | Run non-tx |
| NEVER | Throw exception | Run non-tx |
| MANDATORY | Join outer | Throw exception |

## Impact of Long Transactions

```
           Long Transaction Open (5 seconds)
           ├── Holds ROW EXCLUSIVE lock on orders table rows
           ├── Blocks: other UPDATE/DELETE on same rows
           ├── Holds DB connection from pool
           └── Prevents VACUUM from cleaning up dead tuples
```

## Pattern: Compute Before Transaction

```java
// BAD: fetches data, processes for 2 seconds, then writes — all in one tx
@Transactional
public void processOrderBatch() {
    List<Order> orders = orderRepo.findPendingOrders();  // tx starts here
    // 2 seconds of processing...
    orders.forEach(o -> {
        BigDecimal tax = taxService.calculate(o);  // might be slow
        o.setTaxAmount(tax);
        orderRepo.save(o);
    });
}  // tx ends here — held for 2+ seconds

// GOOD: separate read from write
public void processOrderBatch() {
    List<Order> orders = orderRepo.findPendingOrders();  // read outside tx
    Map<Long, BigDecimal> taxes = new HashMap<>();
    for (Order o : orders) {
        taxes.put(o.getId(), taxService.calculate(o));  // compute outside tx
    }
    applyTaxUpdates(taxes);  // write in short tx
}

@Transactional  // only the write is in the transaction
public void applyTaxUpdates(Map<Long, BigDecimal> taxes) {
    taxes.forEach((id, tax) -> orderRepo.updateTax(id, tax));
    // Transaction is open for milliseconds, not seconds
}
```

## Detect Long Transactions

```sql
-- Find transactions open longer than 5 minutes
SELECT pid, usename, query, state,
       NOW() - xact_start AS duration
FROM pg_stat_activity
WHERE xact_start IS NOT NULL
  AND NOW() - xact_start > INTERVAL '5 minutes'
ORDER BY duration DESC;

-- Terminate a runaway transaction (use with caution)
SELECT pg_terminate_backend(pid) FROM pg_stat_activity
WHERE NOW() - xact_start > INTERVAL '30 minutes';
```

## Statement Timeout as Safety Net

```yaml
# Prevent any single query from running more than 30s
spring:
  jpa:
    properties:
      hibernate:
        javax.persistence.query.timeout: 30000  # 30s
```

```sql
-- Per-session timeout
SET statement_timeout = '30s';
```

## Self-Invocation Trap

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {

    // This method calls the one below — but no transaction is active!
    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        this.processAndSave(order);  // direct call, bypasses proxy!
    }

    @Transactional
    public void processAndSave(Order order) {
        // No transaction here when called from above!
        orderRepo.save(order);
    }

    // FIX: use propagation or inject self via ApplicationContext,
    // or restructure so the public entry point has @Transactional
    @Transactional
    public void placeOrderFixed(Order order) {
        orderRepo.save(order);  // transaction active — method IS the entry point
    }
}
```

## Checked Exception Rollback

```java
// BAD: checked exception does NOT trigger rollback by default
@Transactional
public void transfer(Long from, Long to, BigDecimal amount) throws InsufficientFundsException {
    accountRepo.debit(from, amount);
    if (insufficientFunds()) {
        throw new InsufficientFundsException();  // tx COMMITS here! debit stays!
    }
    accountRepo.credit(to, amount);
}

// GOOD: explicit rollbackFor
@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
public void transfer(Long from, Long to, BigDecimal amount) throws InsufficientFundsException {
    // Now both checked and unchecked exceptions trigger rollback
}
```

## readOnly = true Optimization

```java
// Disables dirty checking, enables read replica routing
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public List<ProductDTO> getAllProducts() {
    return productRepo.findAll().stream()
        .map(mapper::toDTO)
        .toList();
    // Hibernate skips dirty-check snapshots for all loaded entities
}
```

## LazyInitializationException Fix

```java
// Problem: accessing lazy collection outside active session
@Transactional(readOnly = true)  // keep transaction open for lazy loading
public UserDTO getUserWithOrders(Long userId) {
    User user = userRepo.findById(userId).orElseThrow();
    user.getOrders().size();  // triggers lazy load — transaction is still open, works!
    return mapper.toDTO(user);
}
// Or: use @EntityGraph / JOIN FETCH to load eagerly in the query
```
